# Changelog — host companion

## 1.1.0 (2026-09-28)

- perf: `--gzip` on `status`, `transcript`, `usage`, `digest`, `turns` and `diff`: a reply over 4 KB prints as `{"encoding":"gzip","data":"<base64>"}`. On development-central's real data: `usage --days 31` 63.8 KB → 16.7 KB, a first `transcript` page (256 KB tail) 52 KB → 17.5 KB on average; about 1 ms of host CPU. Older companions ignore the flag and answer plain JSON.
- perf: an `events` backlog sends only the newest change of each session (each change carries the whole agent, and the phone keeps only the last one anyway). A phone back after a few minutes with 20 busy agents gets 20 lines instead of one per tool call: 50.5 KB → 15 KB on the benchmark's 80-change backlog, and at most one agent's worth per session however long the backlog.
- perf: `status` carries an `etag`; `status --etag <etag>` answers only `{version, seq, etag, unchanged: true, source, capabilities}` while nothing changed, so the phone's 15 s background poll of an idle machine is ~150 bytes instead of the whole state (15.5 KB for 20 agents). The etag names the daemon instance too, so a restart (same `seq`, pending requests dropped) never matches. Older companions ignore the flag and answer in full.

## 1.0.0 (2026-09-27)

- feat: `usage --from YYYY-MM-DD --to YYYY-MM-DD` and `--day YYYY-MM-DD` answer any range of the kept days (`--days` and `--since` still work; `--days` now goes to 62). The reply adds `to`, `detailFrom`, `historyFrom` and `utcOffsetMin`; `today` totals are today's whatever the range. Older companions ignore the flags, so the phone sends `--days 31` alongside.
- feat: `usage --hourly` splits rows by local hour (`hour`, `hourly: true`); `usage --sessions` adds `bySession` (per day and session, subagents counted for their parent). Both from the cache: hour and session sums for the last 31 days, daily sums kept to 62 days so a 30-day range has a previous period.
- feat: with cswap, rows carry `account`: the account the companion last saw active before the message (sightings in the usage cache, noticed each time `usage` runs). None before the first sighting.
- perf: the cache (version 2) grew from 1.20 MB to 1.35 MB on development-central (1,053 transcripts, 2.2 GB); a warm call stays at ~48 ms median. A version 1 cache is read again once after the upgrade (about 40 capped calls on development-central under load 40, answered from the old daily sums meanwhile, `scan.rebuilding: true`). Nothing runs at idle.
- feat: per-turn snapshots (capability `snapshots`). The daemon snapshots the agent's repository at the start of every turn (UserPromptSubmit) and at its end (Stop / StopFailure; an interrupted turn ends at the next prompt's snapshot) as a commit object under `refs/conductore/snapshots/<session>/<turn>/before|after`: tracked and untracked non-ignored files, parent HEAD, built with a temporary index (`GIT_INDEX_FILE`), `add -u`, `update-index --add`, `write-tree`, `commit-tree`, `update-ref`. The branch, HEAD, the index, the stash and the work tree are never touched. Every git runs with `GIT_OPTIONAL_LOCKS=0`, at nice 10, in its own process group, killed after 20 s; one snapshot at a time, queued by the daemon and never awaited by the event path, so the hook is unchanged. Failures are skips with a reason (logged). Skipped: non-git dirs, an index over 16 MB, more than 20,000 or 256 MB of untracked files; untracked files over 32 MB and nested repositories are left out. Records in `~/.conductore/turns.json` (0600).
- feat: `turns <sessionId>`: the recent turns with prompt headline, times, changed files and line counts, snapshot refs, `committed`, `late` (an edit arrived before the "before" snapshot finished), `others` (sessions working in the same repo meanwhile) and `undone`.
- feat: `diff <sessionId> <turn> [--file <path>]…`: per-file unified diff between the turn's snapshots (the work tree for a running turn), capped at 64 KB per file and 1 MB in all; binaries and submodules summarised with their sizes.
- feat: `undo <sessionId> <turn> [--file <path>]… [--dry-run] [--keep-commits]` and `redo <sessionId> <turn>`: restore the work tree (or single files) to the turn's "before" snapshot with `read-tree` / `checkout-index` from a temporary index, deleting files created since; the current state is snapshotted first so `redo` puts it back. Refused while the agent works, and when HEAD moved since the turn started (the agent committed): there is no option that resets a branch; `--keep-commits` restores the files and leaves the commits.
- feat: pruning: the last 50 turns per session and 7 days; sessions quiet for 7 days go with their refs; each repo is swept for snapshot refs older than 7 days at most every 6 h. No reflogs, so `git gc` collects the objects as usual.
- feat: `transcript` carries the agent's `lastEvent` and `lastToolName`, so the phone tells a finished turn (Stop) from a question asked mid-turn.
- perf: on a copy of this repository (1,188 files) at load 75-95 on 12 cores: 40 ms CPU per snapshot, 210-230 ms wall at nice 10 one after another and 630-660 ms while turns arrive every 0.6 s; one file changed adds four loose objects (24 KB on disk), an unchanged tree only a commit. Hook latency, UserPromptSubmit through the sh hook, 30 turns each, runs interleaved: median 33.6 / 24.0 ms before, 25.3 / 23.8 ms with snapshots (noise: the hook did not change). The daemon's own CPU rises from 190 to 660 ms per 30 turns (starting the gits).

## 0.9.0 (2026-09-27)

- feat: `digest [--since <ms>] [--summaries] [--max-agents 10] [--max-ms 30000] [--lang en|pt] [--stuck-…]` for the phone's agents dashboard (capability `digest`). Per agent since a time: turns, edited files, lines +/- (`git diff --numstat` of those files, 1.5 s cap, else an estimate from the edits), test runs and the last result, failed commands, time waiting on the user, tokens and estimated cost (from the last 2 MB of the transcript), state, project, machine, last activity and the last reply's first line. Stuck flags with a reason: working 30 min without an edit, the same failure 3 times, the same command 5 times, an approval waiting 60 min, a turn that ended on an API error (thresholds per call).
- feat: the daemon keeps a per-agent activity log for it (`~/.conductore/activity.json`, 0600): compact entries from the hook events it already parses, never tool input; at most 300 entries per agent and 64 agents, kept 24 h after an agent's last entry (so agents pruned after they ended still show). Written with the state snapshot; nothing at idle.
- feat: `digest --summaries`: a one- or two-sentence summary per agent whose activity changed since its last one (never on a schedule; only when asked). Same lock-down as `summarize` and `guide` (`claude -p --tools "" --safe-mode --no-session-persistence --model haiku`, fixed system prompt and `--json-schema`, input on stdin between random delimiters, thinking off, nice 10, own process group, one run at a time via `digest.lock`); five agents per call, two calls at once, a 30 s total cap; each agent's facts, previous summary, last prompt and last three replies since that summary (12,000 characters per agent at most). Rolling summaries and today's token use in `~/.conductore/digest.json` (0600, 64 agents, pruned after 24 h); the token use is reported per run and in `usage` as `companion.digest`. Without `--summaries` the facts come back at once with cached summaries and `summaryPending` marks.
- perf: measured on development-central with ten synthetic agents that all changed: facts alone 70 ms; `--summaries` two Haiku calls in parallel, 5.9 s wall, 6.3k tokens (5.7k in, 0.6k out, $0.009) with short replies and 13k tokens ($0.028) with every reply near its 1,500-character cap; a second run with nothing new makes no call (60 ms).
- feat: `guide` can answer `catchUp` ("catch me up": the phone speaks its agents dashboard).
- feat: `install` also registers PostToolUseFailure and StopFailure (async) when `claude --version` is at least 2.1.119 and 2.1.78 (older Claude Code ignored the whole settings.json over an unknown hook event name before 2.1.101); unknown version or no claude: neither. `install` reports `skipped` with the reason and takes them off after a downgrade, `doctor` reports `optional hooks`, `uninstall` removes them. Without them `digest` reads failures and API errors from the transcript tail (`sources`). `status` agents carry `lastError` (`{type, at}`) after a StopFailure, until the next prompt; StopFailure moves the agent to `waiting_input` like Stop.
- fix: the prompt relay (`send`, `interrupt`, `focus`) works for an agent in a named (non-default) Herdr session. The hook passes `$HERDR_SOCKET_PATH`, the daemon records it as the agent's `herdr.socket`, and every herdr command for that agent (the M18 `pane list` check, `agent prompt`, `pane send-text` / `send-keys`, `agent focus`, and the location lookup) goes to that server. Records without it use the default server, as before, and are still checked pane by pane.
- fix: `transcript` returns prompts typed while the agent was working. Claude Code absorbs them into the running turn and records them only as a `queued_command` attachment (`commandMode: prompt`, `origin.kind: human`), never as a `user` line, so the chat view never showed them although the agent read them. They now come back as `user` entries with `queued: true`; queued task notifications and teammate or coordinator messages are still dropped.
- fix: `transcript` no longer skips a line longer than `--max-bytes`. A prompt with pasted images is a line of several MB (the image bytes), so it was dropped whole and never reached the chat view; such a line is now read on its own and reduced as usual (images dropped), with `oversized: true`. Only a line over 64 MB is still skipped.

## 0.8.1 (2026-09-27)

- fix: a daemon could run with no `hostd.pid`. The hook and the CLI often start a daemon at the same moment; the loser could read the winner's lock while it was still empty (created, pid not yet written), take it for stale, replace it and then, finding the socket served, remove it. The lock is now published whole (a hard link of a temp file holding the pid), and a stale one is replaced by rename, never removed first, by the one starter that creates `hostd.pid.takeover`; the others see the new holder and exit. A stale lock with a live foreign pid still never blocks the daemon (M17).

## 0.8.0 (2026-09-27)

- feat: `guide [--timeout-ms 15000]`: the phone's voice guide. `{utterance, context}` JSON on stdin (32 KB max; short ids and labels of machines, projects, agents, pending requests and the screen, never transcripts), one action out of a closed list on stdout (`open`, `chat`, `terminal`, `approve`, `deny`, `approveAllSafe`, `trust`, `send`, `read`, `usage`, `home`, `say`) with a short sentence to speak, or `error` (claude-missing, not-logged-in, timeout, busy, failed); exit 0.
- Same lock-down as `summarize`: `claude -p --tools "" --safe-mode --no-session-persistence --output-format json --model haiku`, a fixed system prompt and `--json-schema`, the request on stdin between random delimiters, thinking off, nice 10, its own process group killed on timeout, one call per user at a time (`guide.lock`), nothing logged. Actions naming ids the context does not hold, or missing their text or minutes, come back as a `say` with `rejected`.
- The phone's other commands are unchanged; older phones never call `guide`.
- feat: `usage` reports every Claude account cswap (claude-swap) manages when it is on PATH or at `~/.local/bin/cswap`: `claude.accounts` (slot, alias, label = alias or a masked email, active, disabled, 5h/7d `usedPct` and `resetsAt`, per-model windows, `stale` for last-good data) and `claude.cswap` (`activeSlot`, `fetchedAt`). One `cswap list --json` per minute at most (3 s timeout, own process group, nice 10), cached in `~/.conductore/cswap-cache.json` (masked rows only), silent fallback. Without cswap neither field exists.
- feat: `cswap-switch <slot> | --best` runs `cswap switch` (or `--strategy best`) for new Claude sessions on the machine and reports the from/to slots by label. Emails are never printed.
- feat: smart approvals (capability `smart-approvals`, reported by `status` and `version`). Every pending request carries `risk` (`low` / `medium` / `high` plus a one-line reason from a pure classifier, `lib/risk.js`), `batchable`, `suggestedRules` and `repo`.
- feat: approval rules in Claude Code's `Tool(pattern)` syntax with a `session`, `repo` or `any` scope and an optional expiry or end-with-session, stored in `~/.conductore/rules.json` (0600, never in Claude Code's settings). The daemon answers a covered request at once, before any tmux/Herdr lookup, and logs it in `~/.conductore/auto-approved.json` (24 h). High risk is never auto-approved.
- feat: `trust <requestId> [--minutes N | --until-session-end | --forever] [--scope repo|session|any] [--rule …]`, `rules [list|add|edit|remove]`, `approve-low [--ids …] [--session …]`, `approvals [--hours 24]`, `classify` (no daemon). `trust` without `--rule` saves a rule for exactly that call, never broader. `always` on a high-risk request is a one-time allow. The auto-approved log holds no tool input and is capped at 500 entries and 128 KB.
- perf: auto-approve latency measured by `host/bench.sh` (25.5 ms per request vs 13 ms for a plain event on development-central at load 80; about 2x a plain event).
- fix (H2): the daemon no longer aborts at its heap limit when a few long permission prompts wait during heavy subagent activity. The long-poll buffer keeps each change serialized once and is capped at 2 M characters as well as 1000 changes (an older cursor gets a snapshot, as after a restart); the old-space limit is 64 MB instead of 16 (a ceiling: idle RSS is unchanged); the daemon's stderr goes to `hostd.log`, so a fatal V8 error leaves a trace; any exit that still runs JS removes `hostd.pid`.
- fix (M17): a stale `hostd.pid` whose pid now belongs to another process (after a reboot or a crash) no longer blocks the daemon. The daemon sets its process title to `conductore-hostd`; the sh clients check `/proc/<pid>/comm` besides `kill -0`, and the daemon checks the command line (`ps` without `/proc`) before it gives up the lock.
- fix (M19): the socket is always `~/.conductore/hostd.sock`, beside the lock, instead of depending on the starter's `XDG_RUNTIME_DIR` (a tmux server started from cron or su made `events` fail and `status` serve a stale snapshot). The CLI still reaches (and `install` still stops) a 0.7 daemon listening in `$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/conductore`.
- fix (M20): agents that never send SessionEnd (Claude Code killed, crashed, SSH dropped, a reboot) no longer stay forever. The hook passes Claude Code's pid (`claude_pid`, its parent or grandparent through `sh -c`, read from `/proc` without a fork); the daemon records it with its start time as `process` when it is Claude Code and ends the agent (`reason: expired`) once it is gone, checked at start and on `status` / `events`. Without `/proc`, an agent ends after 24 h without an event.
- fix (M18): the prompt relay (`send`, `interrupt`, `focus`) can no longer type into the wrong pane. Agents' `tmux` records carry the server `socket` (from `$TMUX`) and the `panePid`; every tmux command goes to that server (`-S`), and right before typing the pane must still run that process, a Herdr pane must still run this Claude session (`herdr pane list`), and Claude Code must still run. Otherwise nothing is typed and the error says why. Records from older daemons are refused until the agent's next event.
- fix (M22): `install` / `uninstall` rewrite `~/.claude/settings.json` through its symlink (a dotfiles link stays a link and its target is updated) and keep the file's mode, 0600 when new, instead of the process umask (a 0600 file holding an API key became 0644).
- fix (L18): a PermissionRequest spool file over 8 MB is answered at once (the terminal asks) instead of leaving its hook waiting the full timeout; the daemon reads only its header and logs the drop. An unparseable one already was.
- fix (L20): two `summarize` calls clearing the same stale lock can no longer both run `claude -p`: the lock is created with its pid in one step (hard link of a finished temp file), only the caller that creates a takeover marker for a stale holder may drop that lock, after checking it still names that holder, and release removes only our own lock.
- fix (L21): `usage --max-ms` now bounds the whole call: it counts from the process start (Node's start and the file walk included), the budget is also checked every 64 lines inside a 4 MB chunk, and scanning stops up to 200 ms early to leave time for saving the cache and the report. On 1,013 real transcripts (cold cache): 2.4-2.5 s per call instead of 2.6-3.2 s for the scan alone.

## 0.7.0 (2026-09-25)

- feat: `summarize [--max-words 45] [--timeout-ms 20000]`: a one- or two-sentence spoken version of the reply on stdin (64 KB max) for the phone's "Claude summary" read-aloud mode, as one JSON line (`summary`, `ms`, `model`, or `error`: claude-missing, not-logged-in, timeout, busy, failed; exit 0). Replies under 40 words come back as they are (`passthrough: true`) without calling Claude.
- It runs `claude -p --tools "" --safe-mode --no-session-persistence --output-format json --model haiku` with a fixed system prompt and the reply on stdin between random delimiters: no tools, no hooks (never seen as an agent), no transcript. Thinking is off (`MAX_THINKING_TOKENS=0`): about 3 s per call instead of 5–55 s. Nice 10, its own process group killed on timeout, one call per user at a time, the text never logged or stored. A CLI one-shot: no daemon change, nothing at idle.
- The phone's other commands are unchanged; older phones never call `summarize`.

## 0.6.0 (2026-09-25)

- feat: `usage [--days 7] [--since <iso>]`: usage at a glance, computed on the host from local files. Claude's 5-hour and 7-day limits (newest statusline reports, remembered after the sessions end), context per live session, and tokens with an estimated cost per local day, project and model from the Claude Code transcripts (`~/.claude/projects/**/*.jsonl`, each message counted once); Codex CLI tokens and rate limits from `~/.codex/sessions` when present (`codex.present: false` otherwise). Costs come from one dated price table (`lib/pricing.js`) and are labelled an estimate (API-equivalent on subscription plans).
- perf: the scan is incremental (per-file offsets and daily sums in `~/.conductore/usage-cache.json`, last 31 days), capped per call (`--max-bytes` 256 MB, `--max-ms` 2500, then `scan.partial: true`), runs at nice 10 and never involves the daemon's event path. On a machine with 1,137 transcripts (2 GB in 31 days) the first fill takes five calls of 0.5–0.9 s; later calls take ~60 ms.
- The phone's other commands are unchanged; older phones never call `usage`.

## 0.5.0 (2026-09-25)

- feat: `ports [--since <seq>]` lists the TCP ports the user's own processes listen on (dev servers), with process, `label` (vite, next, …), cwd and the `seq` at which each first appeared, for the phone's "Preview ready" chip. Computed on request from `ss -ltnpH` (else `lsof`, else `/proc/net/tcp`), reused for 2 s, remembered in `~/.conductore/ports.json`; system ports and daemons are left out. No daemon change; older phones never call it.

## 0.4.0 (2026-09-25)

- perf: the hook (`conductore-hook`) and the statusline (new `conductore-statusline`) are POSIX sh scripts: no Node start per event. An event is spooled as one file (`cat` + `ln`) in `~/.conductore/spool`, which the daemon watches; 2.4 ms and 1.9 MB peak per event instead of 34 ms and 46 MB. PermissionRequest waits on a FIFO the daemon answers (same decision JSON, same "always" rules, same timeout behaviour).
- perf: statusline reports wake the daemon at most once per 10 s per session (hold + parked reports, the last value is never lost); the default line is built without Node.
- perf: daemon diet: `--max-old-space-size=16 --max-semi-space-size=1 --lite-mode --v8-pool-size=1` (4 threads instead of 7, 47 MB RSS / 7.4 MB private instead of 54 / 8.8), no periodic timers (the 5 min prune interval is now a one-shot at the next deadline), snapshot debounce 1 s, per-event log lines only with `CONDUCTORE_LOG=debug`, idle exit after 6 h (`CONDUCTORE_IDLE_EXIT_S`).
- feat: tmux location is resolved by the daemon (cached 5 s per pane); `install` records the node path for the sh clients and migrates 0.3 settings (Node hook entries, `conductore-hostd statusline --chain`); `doctor` reports hook latency and daemon memory; `host/bench.sh`.
- fix: a starting daemon never takes over a socket another live daemon serves, and a custom `CONDUCTORE_HOME` keeps its socket inside it (a test daemon could unlink the real one).
- fix: agents in Herdr get `herdr.workspaceId` / `tabId` / `paneId` from Herdr even when the hook's environment lacks some `HERDR_*` variables (one cached `herdr pane list` in the daemon, matched by pane id or Claude session id); the cwd is never a location.
- The phone's CLI contract (`status`, `events`, `transcript`, `send`, `interrupt`, `decide`, `focus`, `version`, `doctor`) is unchanged; `status` now also applies events still waiting in the spool.

## 0.3.0 (2026-09-25)

- feat: `statusline [--chain '<cmd>']` records context and rate-limit usage per session (`usage` on each agent, throttled `reason: usage` changes); `install` wires or wraps `statusLine`, `uninstall` restores it, `doctor` reports it.

## 0.2.0 (2026-09-25)

- feat: chat view support: agents carry `transcriptPath`; new `transcript` (incremental, capped JSONL reader with `--since` / `--before` / `--tail-bytes`), `send` (types a prompt into the agent's Herdr or tmux pane, no shell) and `interrupt` (Escape) commands.
- fix: agent names drop the status glyphs Claude Code puts in the terminal title ("⚠ claude", "✳ Fix tests"); a bare "claude" window name falls back to the cwd basename.

## 0.1.0 (2026-09-24)

- feat: conductore-hostd daemon + conductore-hook client: Claude Code hook events -> per-agent state over a unix socket, `status` / `events` long-poll / `decide` / `focus` / `install` / `uninstall` / `doctor` / `stop` CLI for the phone, PermissionRequest answered from the phone (allow / deny / always). Replaces moshi-hook.
